#{[17592186530912 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530914 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530965 :answer/value "t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530970 :answer/value "t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530975 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -88 \\\\ -9 & 40\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531032 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531048 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 63 \\\\ -82 & -102\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531067 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531105 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531118 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -88 \\\\ -9 & 40\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531143 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}30 & 63 \\\\ -82 & -102\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531152 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531160 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531169 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531171 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531179 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 63 \\\\ -80 & -102\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531183 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531195 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -88 \\\\ -9 & 30\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531224 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531240 :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})"] [17592186531246 :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531248 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531256 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531263 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 30 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531275 :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531295 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -87 \\\\ -9 & 40\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531307 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531314 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531320 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531340 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -88 \\\\ -10 & 40\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531345 :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531372 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 64 \\\\ -82 & -102\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531563 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{3},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[3,\\,1]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531563 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -9 \\\\ 13 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531566 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & z(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186531566 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531573 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{f(t)},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{-4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{4}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531573 :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4t}\\cdot (-2{e}^{2t})-0\\cdot {e}^{2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{-6t}}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -2{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531575 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531575 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ -3\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2b \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2b+1 \\\\ 2a+5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2b+1=-3a \\\\ 2a+5=-3b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n-2b+1=-3a\\,⇒\\,a=\\frac{2b-1}{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\frac{2b-1}{3}\\right)+5=-3b\\,⇒\\,4b-2+15=-9b\\,⇒\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na=\\frac{2(-1)-1}{3}=\\frac{-2-1}{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186531577 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531577 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531578 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$2$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    .\n  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$1$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531578 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-({a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}) \\\\ {a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{2}+t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-{a}_{2}+2){t}^{2}+(-{b}_{2}+1)t+(-{c}_{2}+3) \\\\ ({a}_{1}-1){t}^{2}+({b}_{1}+2)t+{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coefficients of like powers of $t,$ we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{2}+2=0 \\\\ -\\,{b}_{2}+1=2{a}_{1} \\\\ -\\,{c}_{2}+3={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1}-1=0 \\\\ {b}_{1}+2=2{a}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first and fourth equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2,\\,{a}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the second and fifth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=-1,\\,{b}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these last values into the third and sixth equations, and solving for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=1,\\,{c}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+2t-1 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-t+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186531581 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2/5 & -6/5 \\\\ -6/5 & -7/5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [17592186531581 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [17592186531583 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}^{3}-5{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}^{5} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix."] [17592186531584 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-2.\n$$"] [17592186531589 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix."] [17592186531590 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=1\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-4)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186531594 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-2)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 5+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186531597 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}6{y}_{1}+1 \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix."] [17592186531598 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531598 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2}-{0}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & -2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531600 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{2},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,0]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531600 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 13 \\\\ 6 & -15 \\\\ 16 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531611 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531611 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{3} & 3{y}_{1}^{2}{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot (-1) \\\\ -{1}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(-{x}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(-{x}_{1}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531614 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & z(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186531614 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 30 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531615 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}){e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [17592186531615 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [17592186531620 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & z(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186531620 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-19 & 25 \\\\ 9 & -11\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -88 \\\\ -9 & 40\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531621 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}^{2}\\le {x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  in turn, $Q(x)\\le 1.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $1.$ But before we conclude that $1$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=1.$\n\nNotice that when $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(1)^{2}-(0)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $1.$"] [17592186531627 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{3},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[3,\\,-2]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531627 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 23 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531633 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & z(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186531633 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ {y}_{2} & {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531637 :question/problem "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & z(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}5{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{y}_{1}+6{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186531637 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 21 \\\\ -2 & -24\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 63 \\\\ -82 & -102\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531641 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix."] [17592186531645 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531645 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1) \\\\ 1-3(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531652 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186531660 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186531660 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}_{2} & 2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1) \\\\ 3(-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -24 \\\\ 24 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186531937 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531972 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531979 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186531988 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532008 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532048 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532058 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -10 & 30\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532066 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532068 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-35 & 55\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532073 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}120 & 0 & 180\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532076 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532083 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532112 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532141 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532143 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532145 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532159 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 34\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532164 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532173 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ -68\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532178 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532183 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186532187 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\